<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The entropy change (\(\Delta S\)) for the reaction is calculated using the formula:
\[
\Delta S = \sum n_f \Delta S_f - \sum n_r \Delta S_r
\]
where:
- \(n_f\) is the stoichiometric coefficient of the final products.
- \(n_r\) is the stoichiometric coefficient of the reactants.
- \(\Delta S_f\) is the standard entropy change of formation for each substance.

For the given reaction:
\[
2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g)
\]

The entropy change is:
\[
\Delta S = (2 \times 66.5) + (2 \times 248.2) - (2 \times 91.2) - (3 \times 205.1)
\]
\[
\Delta S = 133 + 496.4 - 182.4 - 615.3
\]
\[
\Delta S = 629.4 - 797.7
\]
\[
\Delta S = -168.3 \text{ J/K mol}
\]

Thus, the entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-168.3 \text{ J/K mol}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

