1. **Anode (oxidation):** The current flows from the cathode (where reduction occurs) to the anode (where oxidation occurs). In this cell, copper will be oxidized at the anode.
   \[
   \text{Cu} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^+ + e^- \quad \text{E}^\circ = 0.342 \text{ V}
   \]

2. **Cathode (reduction):** Silver will be reduced at the cathode.
   \[
   \text{Ag}^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag} \quad \text{E}^\circ = 0.7996 \text{ V}
   \]

The cell potential (\( E^\circ \)) is calculated as:
\[
E^\circ = E^\circ_{\text{cathode}} - E^\circ_{\text{anode}} = 0.7996 \text{ V} - 0.342 \text{ V} = 0.4576 \text{ V}
   \]

Thus, the potential of the cell is \(\boxed{0.4576 \text{ V}}\).
