where:
- \( r = 0.211 \, \text{nm} = 0.211 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \).
- \( \epsilon_0 = 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{C}^2/\text{N} \), the vacuum permittivity.
- \( \pi_0 = 13.6 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \), the elementary charge.
- \( Z_1 = 2 \), the charge of the cation (Ca²⁺).
- \( Z_2 = -1 \), the charge of the anion (Cl⁻).

The Born-Lande energy correction is given by:

B = (4\pi\pi₀ * e^2) / (4\varepsilon_0 * r)

The lattice energy is then:

Lattice Energy = B * (Z_1^2 * Z_2^2)

Given the bond distance \( r = 0.231 \, \text{nm} = 0.231 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \) and the Born-Linde term \( B = 2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm} \), we can calculate the lattice energy as:

Lattice Energy = B * (Z_1^2 * Z_2^2) = (2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm}) \times (2^2 \times (-1)^2) = (2.21 \times 10^{-19}) \times 4 = 8.84 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}

