CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the formula:

ΔH = ΔH°(products) - ΔH°(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy of formation for each substance.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpy of formation (ΔH°) values are:

ΔH°(CaO) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°(H₂O) = -285.8 kJ/mol
∆H°(Ca(OH)₂) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH):

∆H = ΔH°(Ca(OH)₂) - [ΔH°(CaO) + ΔH°(H₂O)]
∆H = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - [(-635.1 kJ/mol) + (-285.8 kJ/mol)]
∆H = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - (-920.9 kJ/mol)
∆H = -986.2 kJ/mol + 920.9 kcal/mol
∆H = -65.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction is -65.3 kJ/mol.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

