\[
\boxed{y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)}
\]

This equation is derived from the slope-intercept form of a line \( y = mx + c \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( c \) is the y-intercept. The slope of the tangent line at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is the derivative of \( f \) evaluated at \( a \), which is \( f'(a) \).

The line passes through the point \( (a, f(a)) \), so we substitute \( x = a \) and \( y = f(a) \) into the slope-intercept form:

\[
f(a) = f'(a)(a) + c
\]

Solving for \( c \), we get:

\[
c = f(a) - af'(a)
\]

Substituting \( m = f'(a) \) and \( c = f(a) - af'(a) \) into the slope-intercept form, we obtain the equation of the tangent line:

\[
y = f'(a)(x) + f(a) - af'(a)
\]

This can be rewritten as:

\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
