<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we need to find the time dilation experienced by the clock on the spaceship. Since the clock in the cockpit and the clock in the carger book are in the same frame of reference, they will experience the same time dilation. Therefore, the time difference between the clocks is negligible. The time on the clock in the cargo bank will be the same as the clock in the cockpit. 

The time dilation formula is given by:

Δt = Δt' / sqrt(1 - v²/c²)

where Δt is the time experienced by the observer in the Earth frame, Δt' is the time experienced by the observer in the spaceship frame, v is the velocity of the spaceship, and c is the speed of light.

We are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years (Δt') when the ship returns to Earth. We are given that the spaceship's velocity is 0.6c (v). We want to find the time experienced on Earth (Δt).

First, we need to calculate the square root of (1 - v²/c²):

√(1 - v²/c²) = √(1 - (0.6c)²/c²) = √(1 - 0.36) = √(0.6) ≈ 0.707

Now, we can find the time experienced on Earth (Δt):

Δt = Δt' / √(1 - v²/c²) = 3 years / 0.707 ≈ 4.24 hours

So, the clock on Earth will show a time of approximately 4.24 hours when the ship returns to Earth.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

