","To calculate the reaction times for a flow tube with a given flow rate and diameter, we can use the formula:

\[
\text{Residence time} = \frac{\text{Flow tube length}}{\text{Flow rate}}
\]

First, we need to convert the flow rate from cm³/min to cm/s:

\[
10 \text{ cm³/min} = \frac{10 \text{ cm}^3}{1 \text{ min}} \times \frac{1 \text{ min}}{60 \text{ s}} = 0.1666 \text{ cm}^3/\text{s}
\]

Next, we need to convert the flow rate to m³/s to match the units of the diameter (0.1 mm = 0.001 m):

\[
0.1666 \text{ cm}^3/\text{s} = 0.1666 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{s}
\]

Now, we can calculate the residence time for each flow tube length (1-10 cm):

1. For 1 cm:
   \[
   \text{Retention time} = \frac{1 \text{ cm}}{0.1666 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{s}} = \frac{0.01 \text{ m}}{0.1666 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{s}} = 60 \text{ s}
   \]

2. For 10 cm:
   \[
   \text{Reaction time} = \frac{10 \text{ cm}}{0.1666 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{s}} = \frac{0.1 \text{ m}}{0.1666 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3/\text{s}} = 600 \text{ s}
   \]

Thus, the reaction times for flow tubes of 1-10 cm downstream from the mixing chamber are:

- For 1 cm flow tube: \(\boxed{60 \text{ s}}\)
- For 10 cm flow tube: \(\boxed{600 \text{ s}}\)

