\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{\tau}
\]

where \(\hbar = \hbar = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} \).

First, we need to convert the lifetime from seconds to seconds: \(2.0 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{s}\).

Next, we need to convert \(\hbar\) from Joules-seconds to electron-electronvolt (eV)-seconds:

\[
\hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} \times \frac{1 \, \text{eV}}{1.604 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}} \approx 6.6 \times 10^{-16} \, \text{eV} \cdot \text{s}
